… a memorial of thanks by Grand Secretary Yang Yiqing 楊一清 and others upon being shown the Imperially Composed Preface to the Collection of Poems in Praise and Harmony with the Grand Secretaries (Yuzhi fuchen zanhe shiji xu 御製輔臣賛和詩集序) [seventh year of the Jiajing 嘉靖 reign, 1528].

Legal Judgments [five items]:
Collecting prohibited books and privately studying astronomy;
Submitting anonymous documents to accuse others of crimes;
While in office, demanding or borrowing property from others;
Wasting labor and effort in gathering materials unfit for use;
Weaving bolts of satin with prohibited dragon and phoenix patterns.

Third Session

Fifth Question [?]:
Question: Since antiquity, sage emperors and enlightened kings, even when presiding over eras of great peace and tranquility, have remained cautious, diligent, and reverently fearful, welcoming and adopting loyal counsels. Tang 唐 [Yao 堯] and Yu 虞 [Shun 舜] are distant indeed, but when we come to the times of the Shang 商 and Zhou 周 dynasties, ministers such as Yi Yin 伊尹, Fu Yue 傅說, and the Duke of Zhou (Jigong 姬公), in advising their rulers, constantly invoked the former kings as warnings and admonitions. Their rulers, in turn, did not hesitate to humble themselves and follow their advice, ultimately becoming exemplary sovereigns of their ages, ascending to supreme governance and consolidating their grand enterprises. In governing, is there truly nothing higher than emulating one's ancestors? After the Three Dynasties, there was one [the Tang Emperor Xuanzong 宣宗] who inscribed the Essentials of Government of the Zhenguan Period (Zhenguan zhengyao 貞觀政要) upon a folding screen, straightening his countenance and folding his hands whenever he read it; and there was one [the Song Emperor Renzong 仁宗] who, when the Precious Instructions of the Three Reigns (Sanchao baoxun 三朝寶訓) was expounded at court, fixed his gaze and inclined his ear, tirelessly inquiring into each matter as it arose. Compared to the Shang and Zhou…

… a memorial of thanks by Grand Secretary Yang Yiqing 楊一清 and others upon being shown the Imperially Composed Preface to the Collection of Poems in Harmonious Response by Grand Secretaries (Yuzhi fuchen zanhe shiji xu 御製輔臣賛和詩集序) [seventh year of the Jiajing reign, 1528].

Legal judgments [five topics]:
Collecting prohibited books and privately studying astronomy;
Submitting anonymous documents to accuse people of crimes;
While in office, soliciting or borrowing property from people;
Wasting labor and effort in gathering materials unfit for use;
Weaving prohibited bolts of satin with dragon and phoenix patterns.

Third Session

Five Policy Questions:
Question: Since antiquity, sage emperors and enlightened kings, even when presiding over an era of great peace and tranquility, have remained cautious, diligent, restrained, and reverent, welcoming and accepting loyal counsels. Tang Yao and Yu Shun are distant in the past, but when we come to the Shang and Zhou dynasties, in the way ministers such as Yi Yin 伊尹, Fu Yue 傅說, and the Duke of Zhou (Jigong 姬公) advised their rulers, they invoked the former kings at every turn as a warning and admonition; and their rulers did not hesitate to humble themselves and follow them, ultimately becoming exemplary sovereigns of their eras, attaining supreme governance and consolidating their grand enterprise. In governance, is there truly nothing more exalted than emulating one's ancestors? From the Three Dynasties onward, there was one who wrote out the Essentials of Government of the Zhenguan Reign (Zhenguan zhengyao 貞觀政要) on a folding screen, always composing his countenance and folding his hands respectfully to read it; and there was one who, when the Precious Instructions of Three Reigns (Sanchao baoxun 三朝寶訓) was presented and lectured upon, fixed his gaze and inclined his ear, tirelessly inquiring into each matter as it arose. Compared to those of the Shang and Zhou…
